    "idea": {
      "short": "1. c4 is the English Opening. It is the fourth most commonly played opening move in chess. By playing this move, White allows the queen to…",
      "wikibookUrl": "https://en.wikibooks.org/wiki/Chess_Opening_Theory/1._c4",
      "wikibookExcerpt": "1. c4 is the English Opening. It is the fourth most commonly played opening move in chess. By playing this move, White allows the queen to move freely and also discourages Black from responding with 1...d5. Additionally, White ensures that the c-pawn will not be blocked behind a knight on c3. The resulting positions often resemble those that arise from 1. d4 openings rather than 1. e4 openings, and the move d4 is frequently played later on. While this opening is not as sharp as 1. e4 or 1. d4, it is still effective in controlling the d5 square and is highly flexible, allowing for easy transpositions into other openings, and is often used when White desires to avoid certain responses to 1. d4 by Black. For example, transposition to Queen's Gambit and Indian Defence positions are very freque",
      "citations": [
        {
          "work": "Wikibooks — Chess Opening Theory",
          "licence": "CC-BY-SA",
          "url": "https://en.wikibooks.org/wiki/Chess_Opening_Theory/1._c4"
        }
      ]
    }
